INT. TAXIDERMY SHOP - BACK ROOM - DAY

Glass eyes everywhere. A stuffed badger on the desk. A deposition table cleared between a moose head and a fox in mid-pounce.

ATTY. CORNELIA BRIGHT, forties, in a grey suit, sits with a legal pad. DESMOND ARCHULETA, fifties, the shop owner, sits across from her in a smock, hands folded.

CORNELIA
State your name for the record.

DESMOND
Desmond Archuleta.

CORNELIA
And you have owned this shop how long?

DESMOND
Twenty-two years.

CORNELIA
And in that time you've never kept a customer's property past the pickup date.

DESMOND
Never.

CORNELIA
Never once.

DESMOND
Not once.

CORNELIA
Mr. Archuleta, how many animals are in this room?

DESMOND
I couldn't say. Thirty. Forty.

CORNELIA
Would it surprise you to learn that forty-one are registered, and forty-two are present?

DESMOND
Counting error.

CORNELIA
Would it surprise you that the forty-second is a recently deceased Great Dane belonging to my client?

Desmond's hands unfold. Cornelia's pen stops. They stare at each other. Behind Desmond, the badger on the desk seems to lean forward.

DESMOND
I want a lawyer.

CORNELIA
I am the lawyer.

They both rise at once. The table scrapes. A glass eye rolls to the floor between them.
